
Let's create a 2-d turn-based space game!  The game should written in Python.  The description of how the game should function is described as follows:

* The main game UI consists of three areas:
	1) a 2-d map on the left half of the UI representing the part of the universe that the player is travelling through with the player's ship at the center of the map.
	2) a ship status UI that occupies the top 2/3 of right hand side of the game's UI.
	3) A command prompt on the bottom 1/3 of the right hand side of the game's UI

* The universe contains the following objects:  stars, planets orbiting stars, black holes, pulsars, worm holes, starbases, and asteroid fields
* The basic unit of distance for the universe is an AU
* The 2-d map should show the objects using graphical symbols that represent those objects
* The 2-d map should show a 20 by 20 AU portion of the universe
* The size of the universe is 1 million by 1 million AU

* The player controls a ship that travels through a fictional universe that is randomly generated each time the game starts that contain the above objects

* The onboard systems of each ship should be the same as the ones available from Star Trek:
	1) shields for defense
	2) phasers and photon torpedos for weapons
	3) sensors for detecting objects in the region of the universe that the player's ship is in
	4) impluse drive for sub light travel
	5) warp drive for faster than light travel
	6) warp core for powering the onboard systems listed in the above

* The ship's vital statistics should be shown graphically in the ship status UI
* The ship's vital statistics are:
	1) ship damage (shown on a scale between 0 and 100 percent)
	2) energy (shown on a scale as 0-100 percent)
	3) shields (shown on a scale between 0 and 100 percent)
	4) operational status of phasers and photon torpedos as a binary indication
	5) the amount of charge in the phasers on a scale of 1 to 100 percent
	6) the number of photon torpedos remaining
	7) warp core temperature (shown on a scale between 0 and 100 percent of max)
	8) the number of crew remaining out of 1000 (the player starts with 1000 when the game begins)
	9) the number of dollars the player has onboard
* Each ship can move by either warp drive or impluse drive according to a specified heading between 0 and 359 degrees
* The enemy players controls a similar type of ship as the player with the same onboard systems
* Enemy ships behave the same as the player's ship
* The player's ship should look like the ship from Star Trek on the map
* Enemy ships should look like Klingon ships on the map
* Each ship is powered by energy carried onboard the ship.  Energy is expressed as percentage between 0 and 100 percent
* Each ship starts the game with 100 percent for energy, shields, and phasers
* Each ship starts with 0 percent damage
* A ship can repair 5% of its damage each turn
* The ship becomes disabled if there are no more crew aboard
* The game is over when the player's ship is either destroyed or disabled

* Shields consume 2 percent of the ship's energy each turn when it is active

* Sensors do not consume any energy to use

* Each ship also starts with 20 photon torpedos
* Ships should not be able to fire phases once the amount of energy reaches zero percent
* Ships should not be able to fire photo torpedos once the amount of energy reaches zero perent or if the number of torpedos remaining reaches zero
* Ships can only fire their phasers once per turn
* Each torpedo does 10% damage to a ship when hit
* Each phaser does 5% damage to a ship when hit
* When fired against a target ship, Torpedos travel 1 AU each turn
* Phasers hit instantly
* Phasers hits randomly between 50 to 100 percentage of the time

* Each ship has an warp core and is governed by the core's temperature
* Warp drives consume 2 percent of the ship's energy each turn when it is active
* Impulse drives consume 1 percent of the ship's energy each turn when it is active
* The warp drive raise the core temperature by 10 percent each turn when in operation
* The impulse drive raise the core temperature 5 percent each turn when in operation
* The core temperature cools down by 5 percent each turn when the warp drive is not active
* The core temperature cools down by 10 percent each turn when the impulse drive is not active
* Warp speed is between 2 and 9 AU per turn
* Ships can warp beyond 9 AUs per turn 
* The impulse speed is 1 AU per turn
* Warp drive and impulse cannot be used simultaneously
* Ships should not be able to move at all once the amount of energy reaches zero percent
* Torpedos have a 1% chance of hitting the warp core if the torpedo hits the target ship
* The ship is destroyed instantly if the warp core is hit

* Enemy ships should appear randomly throughout the game
* At most 3 enemy ships should appear in the area of the universe that the player's ship is located in on the map
* Enemy ships can only first appear within 20 AU of the player's ship

* Stars provide energy for ships
* Stars are where ships can refuel besides starbases
* Stars cannot be destroyed by ships
* Ships needs to be within 1 AU of a star to refuel
* Ships refuel 10% each turn at a star

* Planets can be habited or inhabited.  Habited planets can contain a random number of crew members between 0 to 1000 that are willing to join the player.
* The player cannot recruit new crew members from the planet when the ship's shields are up
* Planets cannot be destroyed by ships

* Black holes destroy ships if they are within 3 AU of the black hole

* Pulsars will disrupt the ship's sensors if the ship is within 2 AU of the pulsar
* When the ship's sensors are disrupted, the objects shown on the 2-d map may be random while the ship is within 2 AU of the pulsar

* Worm holes are paired and they appear randomly throughout the universe
* Worm holes allows ships to travel to the other end of the worm hole within the same turn
* Ships needs to be within 1 AU in order to enter the worm hole
* Ship's energy is not consumed when travelling through a worm hole

* There are two types of starbases:
	1) starbases that are friendly to the player's ship
	2) starbases that are friendly to enemy ships
* The player's ship cannot use starbases that are friendly to enemy ships
* Enemy ships cannot use starbases that are friendly to the player's ship
* The total number of starbases should be 100 in the entire universe and 50% of the total should be friendly to the player and 50% of the total should be friendly to the enemy ships
* Starbases should be randomly positioned in the universe
* Starbases should be greater than 10 AU from one another
* Starbases have the same onboard systems as ships
* Starbases allow ships to be repaired when the ship is within 1 AU of the starbase
* Ships can replenish 25% their torpedos each turn when the ship is within 1 AU of the starbase
* Ships can refuel at a starbase at the rate of 10% energy gained per turn
* Refueling a ship drains the starbase's energy by 1% per turn
* Starbases use shields for defense with phasers and photon torpedos for weapons
* Starbases that are friendly to the player should attack enemy ships that are within 2 AUs of the starbase
* Starbases start with 100 percent energy and energy is replenished 1 percent each turn
* Starbases consume 1 percent of the energy when firing phasers or photon torpedos
* Starbases are destroyed when their damage reaches 100 percent
* Each torpedo costs 50 dollars at a starbase
* Starbases should be shown as an o-ring on the map

* Asteroid fields contain a random number of asteroids in each cluster
* Asteroid clusters can occupy within 5 by 5 AU block on the map
* Each individual asteroid should be shown separately on the map
* Asteroids are where the player can mine for dollars
* The ship has to be within 1 AU of the asteroid to mine it
* Each ship can mine once per turn
* Each mining attempt can generate between 0 to 1000 dollars

* Each turn allows the player and enemies to execute their commands
* When an enemy ship is destroyed, the player's ship receives the cash carried onboard the enemy ship
* The player controls the ship through the command prompt
* Commands that the player can give to the ship should be in natural english familiar to english speakers
* The player can query the status of each object on the map if the player's ship's onboard sensors are operational
* Enemy ships are controlled by OpenAI's GPT-4o large language model
* Enemy ships strategize to destroy the player's ship



